iit-jee-2012-paper-1-offline","paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2012 Paper 1 Offline","permalink":"pthe-colour-of-light-absorbed-by-an-aqueous-solution-of-cu-jee-advanced-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-mziys50tu256jetn","question":{"en":{"direction":null,"content":"<p>The colour of light absorbed by an aqueous solution of CuSO<sub>4</sub> is</p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"orange-red"},{"identifier":"B","content":"blue-green"},{"identifier":"C","content":"yellow"},{"identifier":"D","content":"violet"}],"correct_options":["A"],"answer":null,"explanation":"<p>When we consider the color of light absorbed by a substance, we are referring to the complementary color of the light that is transmitted or reflected by the substance. An aqueous solution of copper(II) sulfate (CuSO<sub>4</sub>) appears blue to our eyes. This color results from the absorption of light in the complementary color range.</p>\n\n<p>The visible spectrum of light can be divided into several regions, each corresponding to a different color. The complementary color of blue is orange-red. This means that the aqueous solution of CuSO<sub>4</sub> absorbs light in the orange-red region of the spectrum.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is:</p>\n\n<p>Option A: orange-red</p>"}},"subject":"chemistry","subjectGroup":null,"timeAllotted":180000,"topic":"important-compounds-of-transition-elements","type":"mcq","year":2012,"isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"yearKey":"iit-jee-2012-paper-1-offline","active":true},{"question_id":"1l23l5xu9","chapter":"aldehydes-ketones-and-carboxylic-acids","chapterGroup":"organic-chemistry","country":"in","difficulty":"medium","exam":"jee-advanced","examGroup":"jee","languages":["en"],"marks":3,"negMarks":1,"paperId":"iit-jee-2012-paper-1-offline","paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2012 Paper 1 Offline","permalink":"pthe-number-of-optically-active-products-obtained-from-the-jee-advanced-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-vjz9i9wy4t74enpu","question":{"en":{"direc